  "query_preview": "..A large MCS is slowly moving northwestward through\nSonora and Santa Cruz County/southern Pima County and scattered\nconvection is moving through Graham County. An outflow recently\npassed through Tucson with gusts 30-40 mph out of the south. IR\nsatellite shows the MCS cloud tops warming, but locally heavy \nrainfall and gusts to 45 mph will be a threat through the night\nand early morning. This overnight activity is being helped by \nan easterly wave that is currently over north central Sonora as \nwell as 0-6 km shear of 25-35 kts and ample moisture. The \neasterly wave will move northward this morning into the \nafternoon through western Pima County and into western Arizona. \nIsolated showers and thunderstorms will likely continue through \nthe morning south and west of Tucson. Hi-res guidance shows the \nconvection over Graham County dissipating over the next few \nhours.\n\nLeft over debris clouds from the MCS will likely inhibit\nconvection from Tucson west this afternoon and evening. HREF and\nREFS members show isolated to scattered convection east of\nTucson with the best chances along the AZ/NM border. The center\nof the upper level high will be over northern New Mexico with\nmid-level flow elevated at 15-20 kts. The environment east of\nTucson will be similar to Monday with PWAT values ranging from \n1.2\"-1.7\", CAPE around 1000 J/kg where there is sufficient \ninsolation to erode any capping, and 0-6 km wind shear of 25-35 \nkts. Because of this any storms do form may have rotating \nupdrafts and be capable severe wind gusts along with heavy \nrainfall. The main uncertainty today will be the extent of storm\ncoverage in the wake of the MCS. Right now the AZ/NM border \nlooks to have the best chances for convection. \n\nWednesday mid-level winds decrease 5-10 kts which also leads to\na reduction in wind shear with 0-6 km shear generally around 20\nkts from Tucson eastward. All other ingredients will be in place\nhowever with similar PWAT values to today and plenty of\ninstability once surface heating starts. Hi-res guidance again\nfavors convection south and east of Tucson with the\ninternational border and White Mountains seeing the best\nchances. Tucson will likely be on the northern periphery of\nstorm chances, but can't rule out an isolated thunderstorm or\ntwo over town. \n\nAfter Wednesday the ridge expands westward and strengthens with\nNAEFS mean 500 mb heights at the 99.5 percentile or max compared\nto the CFSR climatology Thursday into the weekend. Temperatures\nwarm 7-12 degrees above normal during this time frame leading \nto widespread areas of Major to Extreme HeatRisk. Because of \nthis an Extreme Heat Warning is in place Thursday through \nSunday. The two warmest days look to be Friday and Saturday with\nTucson possibly seeing 110 degrees both days. NBM probabilities\nfor Tucson International to hit 110 degrees are 60-70 percent \nboth days. Storm chances and coverage will likely be limited to \nmainly the White Mountains Thursday thanks to the placement of \nthe ridge and loss of moisture. PWAT values decrease by about \n0.25\" and surface dewpoints decrease to 50-55 degrees. Friday \ninto the weekend moisture returns leading to storm chances \nslowly increasing and expanding to central Pima County eastward \nby Sunday into early next week.",
